<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexios I Komnenos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1081">1081</a>–<a href="/years/1118">1118</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexios I Komnenos was Byzantine emperor from 1081 to 1118 and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a>. He restored the empire after defeats against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and Normans, reformed its coinage and finances, and requested Western help that contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. His reign began a period of Byzantine recovery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos.jpg" alt="Alexios I Komnenos" width="279" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; derivative work by Constantine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexios_I_Komnenos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexios was the son of John Komnenos and Anna Dalassene and the nephew of Isaac I Komnenos, emperor from 1057 to 1059. His father had declined the throne when Isaac abdicated, and died a monk in 1067. Alexios and his elder brother Manuel served with distinction against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a>. In 1074 western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under Roussel de Bailleul rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and Alexios subdued them by 1076.</p><p>In 1078 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-iii-botaneiates">Nikephoros III</a> made him commander of the field army in the West, and he defeated the rebellions of Nikephoros Bryennios the Elder at the Battle of Kalavrye and of Nikephoros Basilakes. While troops were assembling against the expected Norman invasion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>, the Doukas faction convinced him to join a conspiracy against Nikephoros III. His mother Anna Dalassene and the empress Maria of Alania played prominent roles in the coup of 1081.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As emperor from 1081, Alexios faced a Norman invasion of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> and his son Bohemond. After initial defeats he allied with the Holy Roman Emperor Henry IV and had recovered most of the losses by 1085. In 1091 he won a decisive victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-levounion">Battle of Levounion</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> with help from Cuman allies. In the later 1090s he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, most of which had fallen to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>. His envoys appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> at the Council of Piacenza in 1095, and Urban called the First Crusade at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a>. It concluded with much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> restored to Byzantine rule.</p><p>He faced more rebellions than any other Byzantine emperor, from pretenders such as the monk Raictor, the Seljuq Turkic emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzachas">Tzachas</a>, who took the title of emperor in 1092, and an impostor claiming to be Leo Diogenes. In 1092 he replaced the debased gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a> with a gold coin of higher fineness, the hyperpyron. In his last twenty years he lost popularity, persecuted followers of the Paulician and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomil">Bogomil</a> heresies, and burned the Bogomil leader Basil at the stake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alexios died on 15 August 1118 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a>, whom he had crowned co-emperor at five in 1092. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a> later wrote the Alexiad on his reign. By allying with powerful noble families, he co-opted most of the nobility into his extended family and government, which gave continuity: every later emperor was related to him by descent or marriage. This undermined bureaucratic effectiveness by placing family connections over merit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexios I Komnenos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the chrysobull of 1082 <b>Alexios I Komnenos</b> granted Venice tax-free trade throughout the empire in return for naval support against the Normans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A chrysobull of the Byzantine emperor <b>Alexios I Komnenos</b> in 1082 granted Venetians tax-free trade throughout the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Manzikert opened Asia Minor to Turkmen settlement and in time helped precipitate the Crusades, as Emperor <b>Alexios I</b>'s appeal to Pope Urban II in 1095 was framed around the Turkish threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet slaves could not legally marry until <b>Alexios I Komnenos</b> allowed it in 1095, and their children were born slaves.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor <b>Alexios I Komnenos</b> and his Cuman allies destroyed an invading Pecheneg horde at Levounion in Thrace, near the mouth of the Maritsa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-29">29 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Byzantine–Norman wars, the Normans of southern Italy under Robert Guiscard, Duke of Apulia and Calabria, defeated the army of Emperor <b>Alexios I Komnenos</b> outside Dyrrhachium, the major Byzantine stronghold in the western Balkans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1099" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexios I Komnenos"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1099">What was here in 1099? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
